      },
      "summary": "The round’s changes address the prior findings about indirect app scopes and extra workflow role assignments. The new scope heuristic can also misclassify a valid app grant on a non-ACR resource as an ACR grant.",
      "findingList": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "backend/AirlineDemo.Api.Tests/ReleaseIdentityContractTests.cs",
          "line": 15,
          "message": "The indirect-scope check counts every local or variable scope as an ACR scope. Limit it to scopes that can be shown to reference the ACR so valid app grants on other resources do not fail AC RELEASE-ID-01.",
          "confidence": 0.91,
          "category": "testing",
          "evidence": {
            "codePathTraced": "AppAcrGrantRoles treats any role assignment for the app identity whose scope is a local or variable as an ACR assignment, without checking whether that scope resolves to azurerm_container_registry.",
            "failureScenario": "If a valid app identity grant targets another resource, such as a storage account through local.storage_scope, the test includes that role alongside AcrPull and fails, even though the app still has exactly one role assignment on the ACR as RELEASE-ID-01 requires."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "agentId": "security-reviewer",
      "reviewType": "security",
      "harness": "codex",
      "harnessVersion": "0.162.0",
      "model": "gpt-6-luna",
      "approved": false,
      "findings": {
        "critical": 0,
        "major": 1,
        "minor": 0,
        "suggestion": 0
      },
      "summary": "The new check catches direct local and variable scopes, but a valid function-wrapped scope can still hide an extra app ACR grant from the tests.",
      "findingList": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "backend/AirlineDemo.Api.Tests/ReleaseIdentityContractTests.cs",
          "line": 54,
          "message": "The reported indirect-scope bypass remains for function-wrapped local or variable scopes. Reject or resolve those expressions so an extra app ACR grant fails the test.",
          "confidence": 0.91,
          "category": "security",
          "evidence": {
            "codePathTraced": "AppAcrGrantRoles counts a role assignment only when its scope contains the literal azurerm_container_registry reference or matches the direct local./var. pattern.",
            "failureScenario": "If an extra app AcrPush assignment sets scope = coalesce(local.acr_scope, \"fallback\"), where local.acr_scope resolves to the ACR ID, neither scope check matches. The C# contract test skips the assignment, and the Terraform checks also miss this indirect scope, so the tests can pass while the app identity gains push access (OWASP A01:2025 Broken Access Control)."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
